        <section id="artefacts-and-packages"  class="t-bg-supporting3">
          
            <h2>Artefacts and packages</h2>
              <p>An artefact is any of the single items that would be part of the software project. A package is a packaged project which would contain the artefacts.   As you can see from the below diagram, different artefacts would be created by different members of the product team at different stages of the project lifecycle.</p>
<p><img src="~/images/svg/cp3/cp3_u5_s1_p2_image.svg" alt=""></p>
<p>Artefacts can also include things like case studies, data models, design documents, scripts, and work products.</p>
<p>Artefacts are like roadmaps which can be used for developers to trace the project process. Artefacts are documented and would be stored in the code repository or in a separate ‘Artefact repository’ – this is a decision that a DevOps engineer would make. Keeping relevant artefacts in a repository helps developers to access artefacts as and when they need them during the project.</p>
<p>Some of the artefacts <strong>explains how</strong> the code is run; but others would <strong>enable the code to run</strong>. For example, one artefact could be a list of resources needed for the project, whilst another could be the project source code.</p>

          
        </section>
        
    
     
    
  
     
    
  
  
