        <section id="packaging-an-artefact" >
          
            <h2>Packaging an artefact</h2>
              <p>An artefacts package would be created during continuous integration and would be part of the build process so it could be included at the deployment stage of the project. An artefact package is a logical package that can assemble all the project artefacts that have been created in a single build (or artefacts created in multiple builds).  When identifying specific artefacts needed for a deployment an artefact version can be created in the manifest. If deployment is to multiple environments, you can also create environment artefact packages so certain artefacts are only deployed to their relevant environment.</p>
<p>The general Continuous Integration (CI) process for traditional application code is:</p>
<ol>
<li>Merge changes to the main source branch from a feature branch</li>
<li>Compile code to releasable form</li>
<li>Run unit tests</li>
<li>Package into a releasable artefact</li>
<li>Publish artefact to an artefact repository</li>
</ol>
<p>As you can see from the diagram, the Artefacts package would be created in step 4.</p>
<p><img src="~/images/svg/cp3/cp3_u5_s1_p2_image1.svg" alt=""></p>

          
        </section>
        
    
     
    
  
     
    
  
  
        <section id="example-of-a-package-management-tool"  class="t-bg-supporting3">
          
            <h2>Example of a package management tool</h2>
              <h3>Docker</h3>
<p>Docker is a container management service. Docker uses an ‘image’ format which stores data and metadata.  A docker image make good release artefacts because the image format is a portable application package.</p>
<p>Using a package management tool such as Docker would expand on the steps 4 and 5 illustrated in the above diagram.</p>
<p>As you can see in the below diagram, custom software is packaged into a Docker image, which is tested, and then published into a releasable artifact.</p>
<p><img src="~/images/svg/cp3/cp3_u5_s1_p2_image2.svg" alt=""></p>
<p>The Docker image builds an artefact that is easy to inspect which means it can be built and tested before it is published. The Dockerfile (which is a software artifact), would contain instructions for packaging an application in a Docker image.</p>

          
        </section>
        
    
     
    
  
     
    
  
  
        <section id="another-example-of-a-package-management-tool"  class="t-bg-primary">
          
